<h2 class="mt-12">Replenishment matchup FAQ</h2>
<section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6">
<div><h3>Klaviyo or Shopify Email for replenishment?</h3><p>Shopify Email is a reasonable start when replenishment campaigns are occasional and the catalog is small. Klaviyo pays off when replenishment work needs event-driven branching, catalog-aware content, and segment-level reporting. Model profile-based billing against expected contact growth before committing.</p></div>
<div><h3>Omnisend vs Klaviyo for replenishment?</h3><p>Omnisend tends to be faster for a small team running email-first replenishment campaigns with light SMS. Klaviyo offers deeper segmentation and event flexibility, which matters as replenishment logic grows. Pilot both with one real replenishment journey and compare maintenance time, not feature lists.</p></div>
<div><h3>Mailchimp or Klaviyo for replenishment?</h3><p>Mailchimp suits teams that value a familiar editor and broad campaign tooling for replenishment newsletters and simple automations. Klaviyo is stronger where replenishment messages depend on Shopify order, cart, and browse events. Check both official pricing pages at your contact volume before deciding.</p></div>
<div><h3>Do I need a separate SMS tool for replenishment?</h3><p>Not at the start. Several platforms cover basic SMS alongside email, and SMS specialists earn their cost only when text messages measurably improve replenishment outcomes. Confirm consent handling, quiet hours, and per-message pricing, and verify that your audience actually responds to SMS.</p></div>
<div><h3>How should I suppress audiences in replenishment flows?</h3><p>Exclude recent purchasers, open support or return cases, refunded orders, and anyone without documented consent. For replenishment, write exit conditions next to each flow so another operator can audit them. Suppression mistakes cost more margin than a missed campaign.</p></div>
<div><h3>What does replenishment email cost?</h3><p>Costs combine the platform subscription, contact or send overages, SMS credits, template and creative work, and the discount budget your replenishment campaigns consume. Providers change plans and limits often, so check official pricing pages and model an eighteen-month total before committing.</p></div>
<div><h3>Which app should a lean team pilot first for replenishment?</h3><p>Start with the tool your team can fully operate in two weeks: native Shopify Email for simple replenishment sends, or a lean ecommerce platform when branching and suppression matter. A completed pilot beats an ambitious setup that stalls during week one.</p></div>
<div><h3>How do I measure replenishment email results?</h3><p>Track margin per send, repeat purchase, unsubscribe and complaint rates, and support load alongside attributed revenue. For replenishment specifically, compare a holdout group against recipients so seasonal lift is not mistaken for program impact.</p></div>
<div><h3>Can I run replenishment email without an agency?</h3><p>Yes, if the scope stays small. Pick one replenishment journey, document consent and suppression rules, and reuse a simple template system. Add outside help only when flow complexity, deliverability remediation, or peak-season volume exceeds in-house capacity.</p></div>
<div><h3>When should I graduate from my first app for replenishment?</h3><p>Graduate when the team cannot safely edit flows, segment reliably by purchase state, or forecast cost at your growing contact count. For replenishment, that moment usually arrives when more than two people maintain flows or when peak campaigns require documented suppression.</p></div>
<div><h3>How much discounting is acceptable for replenishment?</h3><p>Treat discounts as one lever, not the default. For replenishment, test content-led recovery and loyalty first, cap discount depth against margin, and document who can approve exceptions. If most revenue needs a code, the program has a value problem rather than a pricing problem.</p></div>
<div><h3>Which Shopify data matters most for replenishment?</h3><p>Order and refund state, cart and browse events, consent source, and product availability cover most replenishment decisions. Verify each event fires correctly in a test purchase before building logic on top of it, and document field meanings so marketing and engineering agree.</p></div>
<div><h3>How do I avoid duplicate sends across apps for replenishment?</h3><p>Give one platform ownership of each replenishment journey, document which app sends what, and share suppression lists where the tools support it. Run a weekly audit during peak season that samples customers and lists every message they received.</p></div>
<div><h3>What should a replenishment pilot include?</h3><p>A bounded pilot covers one audience, one or two journeys, explicit suppression rules, a holdout group, and a thirty-day review of margin and complaints. Agree on the success criteria before launch so results cannot be reinterpreted afterward.</p></div>
</section>
